# Database Migrations

iVenue uses Alembic for schema migrations. Run all commands from the `backend` directory so Alembic loads the project settings and SQLite database configured in `.env`.

```powershell
cd backend
```

## Create a Migration

After changing a SQLAlchemy model, generate a migration from the model metadata:

```powershell
uv run alembic revision --autogenerate -m "add venues table"
```

Migration filenames are generated automatically using this format:

```text
MYYYYMMDDHHMM_<description>.py
```

Example:

```text
M202610072203_add_venues_table.py
```

Review the generated migration before applying it. Autogeneration should not be treated as a replacement for a migration review.

## Apply Migrations

Apply all pending migrations:

```powershell
uv run alembic upgrade head
```

Apply the next migration only:

```powershell
uv run alembic upgrade +1
```

Apply migrations up to a specific revision:

```powershell
uv run alembic upgrade <revision>
```

## Inspect Migration State

Show the revision currently applied to the database:

```powershell
uv run alembic current
```

Show the latest available revision:

```powershell
uv run alembic heads
```

Show the migration history:

```powershell
uv run alembic history
```

Show the history in more detail:

```powershell
uv run alembic history --verbose
```

Check whether model metadata differs from the database schema:

```powershell
uv run alembic check
```

## Undo Migrations

Undo the most recently applied migration:

```powershell
uv run alembic downgrade -1
```

Undo migrations to a specific revision:

```powershell
uv run alembic downgrade <revision>
```

Undo all migrations and return to an empty schema:

```powershell
uv run alembic downgrade base
```

## Reset Local SQLite Database

For local development only, remove the SQLite database and recreate it from migrations:

```powershell
Remove-Item .\ivenue.db -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
uv run alembic upgrade head
```

Do not use this reset command against a shared or production database. Use a reviewed downgrade or a forward migration instead.

## Baseline an Existing Database

If a database already contains the correct schema but has no Alembic version history, mark it as current without running migrations:

```powershell
uv run alembic stamp head
```

Only use `stamp` after verifying that the existing schema matches the migration history. It does not create or alter tables.

## Seed an Admin Account

Apply the schema first:

```powershell
uv run alembic upgrade head
```

Set the account details in the current PowerShell session:

```powershell
$env:ADMIN_PHONE="your-phone-number"
$env:ADMIN_EMAIL="your-email@example.com"
$env:ADMIN_NAME="Your Name"
$env:ADMIN_ROLE="super_admin"
$env:ADMIN_PASSWORD="your-strong-password"
```

Then seed the account:

```powershell
uv run python scripts/seed_admin.py
```

Allowed roles are `admin` and `super_admin`. Public signup cannot create either privileged role. Development admin login uses the email and password configured above. Phone OTP remains commented/deferred until Termii is approved.

## Initial Migration

The initial authentication schema is:

```text
migrations/versions/M202610070000_initial_auth_schema.py
```

It creates the `users` and `verification_codes` tables used by the authentication flow.
